  "In many cities, small patches of unused land sit between buildings, beside railway lines or along the borders of car parks. For decades these spaces were either covered with concrete or left to fill with litter and weeds. In recent years, however, more and more local groups have started to turn them into what are often called tiny forests: densely planted areas, sometimes no larger than a tennis court, in which dozens of different native trees and shrubs* are grown close together.",
  "The method behind these forests is quite different from the way trees are usually planted in parks. Instead of placing a few large trees far apart on a neat lawn, volunteers first improve the soil and then plant young seedlings only a short distance from one another, mixing tall species with smaller ones that naturally grow beneath them. Because the plants compete for light, they grow upwards quickly, and after two or three years of weeding and watering the forest is usually expected to look after itself. Supporters claim that such a site can become thick and green far sooner than a conventional planting.",
